## Creativity scoring — literature the axes are derived from

The candidate score in `src/katala_research/creativity/score.py` and the
8-dimension report rubric in `src/katala_research/creativity/rubric.py` are this
repository's own formulation. No third-party rubric, sheet, or scoring instrument
is reproduced. Each axis is derived from a published evaluation concept, cited in
the module docstring where it is used:

- Zhai, Cooper & Lafferty (SIGIR 2003) — subtopic retrieval / S-recall
- Clarke et al. (SIGIR 2008) — novelty and diversity, alpha-nDCG
- Zhang, Callan & Minka (SIGIR 2002) — novelty and redundancy detection
- Ziegler, McNee, Konstan & Lausen (WWW 2005) — topic diversification,
  intra-list diversity
- Vrijenhoek et al. (2021) — RADio, viewpoint diversity in news recommendation
- Saracevic (2007) — relevance manifestations, situational relevance / utility
- Rashkin et al. (Computational Linguistics 2023) — Attributable to Identified
  Sources
- Boden, *The Creative Mind* (2nd ed., 2004) — combinational creativity
- Grice, "Logic and Conversation" (1975) — maxim of quantity

These are cited as prior art for the ideas, not copied text. `content_gap` in
`score.py` has no literature basis and is marked in the source as an own axis.

